    "content": "On August 13, 2026, former U.S. President Donald Trump made a statement at Joint Base Andrews in Maryland that has drawn national attention. Responding to a reporter's question about his political future, Trump said he would be open to running for the presidency a third time but conceded that U.S. law presents an obstacle. He noted that many people want him to continue leading the country, but he pointed to the Constitution's restrictions on presidential term limits.\n\nThe U.S. Constitution, specifically the 22nd Amendment, ratified in 1951, states that no person can be elected to the office of the President more than twice. This amendment was adopted after Franklin D. Roosevelt's four-term presidency, establishing a two-term limit as a check on executive power. Legal scholars have consistently interpreted this as an absolute bar to any third term, regardless of whether the terms are consecutive.\n\nTrump's remarks come amid ongoing speculation about his influence within the Republican Party and his potential role in the 2028 election cycle. While some supporters have floated the idea of challenging the amendment through a new constitutional convention, such an endeavor would require unlikely bipartisan support and a lengthy ratification process. No serious legal challenge has been filed, and most experts agree that the amendment's wording is clear.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant because it highlights the stability of U.S. democratic institutions, which many in the community rely on for security and opportunity. Somali Americans, who are active in civic life, often look to U.S. laws as a model for governance. The debate over term limits also resonates in Somalia, where constitutional reforms have been a contentious issue. The diaspora's engagement with U.S. politics may grow as they observe how leaders navigate legal constraints.\n\nIn the coming months, Trump's statement will likely fuel discussions about constitutional interpretation and the limits of executive power. However, without a change in law, a third Trump candidacy appears legally improbable. The 22nd Amendment remains a firm pillar of the U.S. political system, ensuring a peaceful transfer of leadership.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/13/trump-oo-sharciga-u-arka-caqabad-ku-hortaagan-xil-saddexaad/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=trump-oo-sharciga-u-arka-caqabad-ku-hortaagan-xil-saddexaad",
